.TH UDPDIST 1 "2026" "saphira-udpdist" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
udpdist \- Layer 4 UDP distributor for Saphira Linux, DSR/LVS-DR style
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B udpdist
.RI [ options ]
.IR service_name
.br
.B udpdist
.RI [ options ]
.BI \-c " path"
.br
.B udpdist
.B \-\-check
.BI \-c " path"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B udpdist
accepts UDP datagrams addressed to one or more frontend addresses and ports
and redirects each flow, at layer 2, to a backend selected by rendezvous
(rendezvous/HRW) hashing. It is a distributor, not a proxy: no address is ever
rewritten, so the backend observes the real client address and replies to the
client directly, without the distributor on the return path.
.PP
This is the same shape as LVS-DR. Consequences worth understanding before
deploying it:
.IP \(bu 3
The frontend address is the service identity. It must be configured on the
distributor and, as a /32 (IPv4) or /128 (IPv6) on the loopback interface of
every backend.
.IP \(bu 3
Backends must be reachable on the same layer 2 segment. This release does not
tunnel to a backend on a remote segment.
.IP \(bu 3
Nothing is translated, so there is no state table for ordinary UDP flows, no
per-flow descriptor, and no return-path ambiguity. Two datagrams with an
identical 5-tuple are one flow and always go to the same backend.
.PP
Because the layer 3 header is forwarded unchanged, the destination address in
every frame is the frontend address. The backend accepts it because the same
address is local to it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " path"
Read
.I path
instead of
.IR /etc/saphira/udpdist/<service_name>.conf .
The derived service name is validated with exactly the same character set as
one given positionally.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Validate the configuration, print a one-line summary and exit. Opens no
sockets and requires no privilege.
.TP
.B \-\-syslog
Log to syslog instead of standard error.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the configuration. The file is parsed in full first; if anything is
invalid the previous configuration is kept and the failure is logged. Counters
are preserved.
.IP
A reload that would require opening a new layer 2 socket, or binding a port
below 1024, is refused and the old configuration is kept: those operations are
not permitted after the privilege drop, and half-applying a reload is worse
than not applying it. Everything else \(em backends, hardware addresses, health
thresholds, QUIC configuration, and frontends on ports of 1024 or above \(em
applies on the next datagram. There is no address baked into a socket, so a
frontend change cannot desynchronise anything.
.TP
.B SIGUSR1
Write a statistics dump to the log. Every counter is included, together with
the QUIC routing state actually in use for each frontend.
.TP
.B SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Comments begin with
.B #
or
.BR ; .
A line longer than 1023 bytes is rejected rather than silently split.
Unknown keys and unknown attributes are rejected.
.SS frontend
.BI frontend= address : port
.I iface
.I proto
.I quic_mode
.TP
.B iface=\fINAME\fR
Interface carrying the frontend. Optional: if omitted, the interface owning
the address is discovered at startup.
.TP
.B proto=udp|quic
Default
.BR udp .
.B q
uic makes the daemon interpret the connection id where it can. See
.B QUIC
below. It is never inferred: any non-QUIC datagram whose first byte has the
header-form bit clear is byte-identical in shape to a QUIC packet, so a
frontend must say which it carries.
.SS backend
.BI backend= address : port
.I mac
.I sid
.TP
.BI mac= xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx
Required in practice. Layer 2 redirection needs the hardware address, and it
cannot be discovered on the forwarding path. At startup each configured
address is compared against the neighbour table; a mismatch is fatal, because
a wrong hardware address is an invisible blackhole rather than a recoverable
fault.
.TP
.BI sid= N
QUIC-LB server id for this backend. Required when any
.B quic_config
is present, and unique across the pool.
.SS quic_config
.BI quic_config= id
.I cid_len
.I server_id_len
.I self_describing
.TP
.BI quic_config= N
Configuration codepoint, 0 to 6. The first three bits of every connection id
name the configuration. Codepoint 7 is reserved by the load-balancer
specification, always means "unroutable", and is never accepted.
.TP
.BI cid_len= N
Total connection id length in bytes for this configuration. A short-header
packet carries no length field, so the length must come from here; the daemon
never guesses it.
.TP
.BI self_describing= 1
The five low bits of the first connection-id octet encode the length of the
connection id that follows it. Mutually exclusive with
.BR cid_len .
.TP
.BI server_id_len= N
Plaintext server id length in bytes, 1 to 7. This release implements the
unkeyed (plaintext) routing scheme only. Encrypted connection ids are a later
milestone and are not implemented; a pool using them is not supported.
.SS Other keys
.TP
.BI max_datagram= N
Largest datagram accepted, 64 to 65535, default 9000. Larger datagrams are
dropped and counted. A datagram is never truncated, because a truncated
datagram is corruption rather than loss.
.TP
.BI require_mac= 1
Refuse to start unless every backend carries an explicit
.BR mac .
.TP
.BI skip_csum_offload_check= 1
Do not check for checksum offload at startup. See
.BR REQUIREMENTS .
.SH BACKEND ADDRESSES
A backend normally carries an explicit
.BR mac= ,
which udpdist cross-checks against the kernel's neighbour table and refuses to
start if the two disagree.
.PP
.B mac=auto
instead takes the hardware address from that table at startup, and follows it
thereafter: if a backend's address changes, udpdist follows the change and counts
it as
.BR mac_changed ,
rather than continuing to send frames to an address nobody answers.
.PP
.B mac=auto
is only meaningful for a backend on the same broadcast domain. Neighbour
resolution is undefined across a router, and a gateway never answers an ARP
request for an address beyond it, so an off-segment backend marked
.B mac=auto
cannot be resolved by any amount of retrying. udpdist reports that case
explicitly instead of retrying forever: reaching such a backend requires
tunnelled DSR, not a longer timeout.
.PP
A neighbour state udpdist cannot name is refused rather than assumed usable. An
unrecognised value usually means the table is being misread, and installing a
hardware address on that evidence would be guessing; failing closed costs a
startup refusal with a diagnostic, where guessing costs a silent blackhole.

.SH QUIC
.B udpdist
does not terminate TLS and does not parse HTTP/3. It routes QUIC, including
HTTP/3, without understanding it.
.PP
A QUIC connection is identified by its connection id, not by its 5-tuple, and
QUIC is designed to survive the client changing address or port. Hashing the
tuple alone therefore moves a connection to a different backend when the client
rebinds, and the second backend does not hold that connection's state.
.SS quic_mode=fallback
Requires nothing from the backends. Affinity is held by a bounded 4-tuple
table for as long as the client is still using client-generated connection ids,
which is the window before the server issues one of its own. Ordinary HTTP/3
traverses correctly. A source-port rebinding may survive if the datagram still
matches the recorded tuple.
.IP
Genuine address or path migration is
.BR "not"
guaranteed in this mode, and this program makes no claim that it is. IPv4 to
IPv6 migration is not covered.
.SS quic_mode=quic_lb
Every backend must emit routable connection ids following the
.B quic_config
scheme. Affinity is then taken from the connection id, so a connection stays on
its backend across source-port and address changes. This is the mode that earns
the migration-safe claim.
.IP
Both mechanisms apply within a single connection and the progression is
automatic: client-generated connection id, then bounded 4-tuple affinity, then
routable connection id, after which the fallback state expires unused.
.IP
If
.B quic_mode=quic_lb
is configured and the connection-id configuration is missing or invalid,
configuration validation
.B fails
and the daemon refuses to start. It never silently degrades to
.BR fallback ,
and the status output never reports a frontend as QUIC-LB merely because it
carries QUIC traffic.
.SS Backend loss
A new connection is never sent to a backend that is not healthy. An
established connection whose backend has failed is
.B dropped and counted
.RB ( quic_backend_down ).
It is never re-routed: a different server does not hold that connection's state
and would reject the connection id. Clients re-establish on their own.
.SH DEPLOYMENT REQUIREMENTS
.SS On the distributor
.TP
.B Checksum offload must be disabled
on every interface the distributor redirects on:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ethtool -K \fIinterface\fR tx off rx off
.fi
.RE
.PP
The daemon forwards received bytes unchanged, so a transport checksum that
hardware has not finished computing reaches the backend, which discards the
datagram at its own verification. Every packet would be lost while the daemon
reported a successful forward. The condition is checked before the first
packet and is fatal unless
.B skip_csum_offload_check=1
is set.
.TP
The frontend address must be present on the distributor so the host answers for
it. A UDP socket is bound to every frontend address and port purely so the
local kernel considers the service owned and does not emit ICMP
port-unreachable for datagrams being handled elsewhere. That socket is never
read for forwarding, is drained so its receive queue cannot grow, and is one
descriptor per frontend, not per flow.
.TP
.B /etc/saphira
must be traversable and readable by the
.B udpdist
user, or the daemon refuses to start rather than run unable to reload.
.SS On every backend
.TP
The frontend address as a /32 on
.B lo
for IPv4, or a /128 for IPv6. This makes the address local without
advertising it.
.TP
ARP and neighbour announcement suppressed on that address, so the backend does
not answer for the frontend address. Linux:
.BR "arp_ignore=1, arp_announce=2" .
Windows requires the weak-host receive setting
.BR "ipsender=weak" .
BSD equivalents apply.
.TP
A direct route from the backend to the client. The reply never traverses the
distributor, so the backend must be able to reach the client itself.
.TP
For QUIC: a certificate valid for the frontend's hostname, issued by an
authority the client trusts. All backends are indistinguishable to the client,
and a connection that migrates between them verifies the second one's
certificate. Per-backend self-signed certificates do not work.
.TP
Identical QUIC-LB parameters across the pool, including the configuration
codepoint and the server id allocation.
.SH PRIVILEGE
The daemon needs CAP_NET_RAW to open its layer 2 sockets and nothing
afterwards. It then drops supplementary groups, gid and uid to the packaged
.B udpdist
identity and proves the drop by requiring
.B setuid(0)
to fail. No capability is retained, not even CAP_NET_RAW, and there is no
privileged supervisor and no file capability.
.SH STATISTICS
Counters are in memory only and reset on restart. SIGUSR1 prints:
.TP
.B rx
Frames received from the network and accepted for processing.
.TP
.B fwd
Datagrams handed to a backend. This is
.B "accepted by the kernel",
not a delivery receipt; loss after the send is invisible here and is inherent to
an unacknowledged datagram.
.TP
.B drops
non_ip, not_udp, malformed, truncated, oversized, bad_cksum, multicast,
broadcast, not_ours, no_backend, send_failed, and self_echo, which counts
frames this host itself emitted and deliberately ignored.
.PP
Fragment state is reported as frag initial, subsequent, orphan, table_full.
Every received frame has exactly one disposition, so
.B rx
equals
.B fwd
plus the sum of the drop counters.
.SH LIMITS
.TP
.B Frontends per service
32
.TP
.B Backends per service
64
.TP
.B Interfaces per service
8
.TP
.B Fragment associations
4096, short lived; a full table is counted and the datagram dropped
.TP
.B QUIC fallback affinities
4096, short lived
.TP
.B Service name
.Letters, digits, underscore and hyphen only
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on a configuration or startup failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ping (1),
.BR ip (8),
.BR ethtool (8)
.PP
RFC 9000 (QUIC transport), draft-ietf-quic-load-balancers (routable connection
ids).
